\details{
\subsection{Sub-Object Initialisation}{

All sub-object slots are initialised to empty objects of the appropriate
class when not supplied. Empty sub-objects cause the model to skip
observation error for that data type (e.g., an empty \code{Landings} slot means
catch is reported without observation error). To activate observation error
for a data type, supply a sub-object with at least \code{CV} specified.
}

\subsection{Pass-Through Access}{

When \code{Name} is an \link[=om-class]{om}, \link[=hist-class]{hist}, or \link[=mse-class]{mse} object, \code{Obs()}
extracts the \code{Obs} slot of the embedded OM rather than constructing a new
object:
\itemize{
\item \code{Obs(om)} → \code{om@Obs}
\item \code{Obs(hist)} → \code{hist@OM@Obs}
\item \code{Obs(mse)} → \code{mse@OM@Obs}
}

The result in all cases is the two-level named list
\verb{[[stock_complex]][[fleet_name]]} of \link[=obs-class]{obs} objects stored in the OM.
}

\subsection{Attaching to an OM}{

A single \link[=obs-class]{obs} object can be assigned to a specific stock complex and
fleet slot with:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{Obs(om) <- MyObs           # replaces the full Obs list
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Individual sub-objects are more commonly set directly on the \code{obs} object
before it is inserted into the OM.
}
}
